From: noname <noname@example.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 23:20:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] 0039 name the queue the embedder feeds and count what it takes

The window says it pressed, the drain says it forwarded nothing, and both are
true. `play-d2-1.log` (the r0.65 attempt that launched Destiny 2 by itself
through `--launch-app-id=100362311`) is the cleanest run this series has
produced so far: the window opened by itself 132 s in, the streamer's input
gate was already open, and the HID wire was finally visible because
`OPENNOW_NVST_TRACE=1` arms `verbose_diagnostics_enabled()` (`nvst.rs:198`)
- without that variable a count of 0 for the wire markers proves nothing,
which is what the last two sessions got wrong.

  23:08:01.146 wayland-video: stream window created
  23:08:01.171 wayland-video input: capture active        (never inactive again)
  23:09:01.146 wayland-video input: opening enter answered (14 left)
  23:09:01.298 wayland-video input: opening enter held for 150 ms and released
  ... 4 presses, 5 s apart, 0 of them refused
  NVST native input drain: available=true clearedPolls=2906 keys=0 absolutes=1 moves=0 buttons=0
  NVST native input rx: events=[mouse-settings(25)]
  NVST encoded input tx: route=ControlReliable
  NVST native input rx: events=[mouse-absolute(5)]
  NVST encoded input tx: route=ControlPartial

So the drain's queue is live and shared: the one absolute the seat made at the
window's own creation came through and was encoded onto the wire. The four
Enters the window answered Steam's pop-up with never reached `queue_input` at
all, and `submit` returns nothing, so a press that `EmbeddedInputCapture`
refuses (`embedded_input.rs:139: if self.active.load(Ordering::Acquire)`) is
silent even though the surface asked for capture and the streamer accepted it.
Nothing in the middle says which queue the window wrote and whether it took the
event.

This counts both sides of that gate and prints the queue's address:

  NVST embedded input tally: active={} pushed={} dropped={} keys={} moves={}
      absolutes={} buttons={} other={} queue={:p}

once per 24 events, and puts the same address beside the drain's counters, so
the next session can say in one line whether a press entered the queue the
drain reads, entered a queue nobody reads, or was dropped because capture was
inactive. The counters are statics of `AtomicUsize` and the drain gains one
`format!` per 5 s line; nothing is buffered.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [STM32MP25 performance work]

